# Fix PWA Update Fallback Logic

This plan replaces the brittle `localStorage` and Service Worker hash comparison fallback with a robust build-time timestamp approach. This guarantees that if a tablet or browser is stuck on an old version of the app, it will persistently detect the new version until it successfully updates.

## Proposed Changes

### 1. Update Vite Configuration
We will inject a unique build timestamp into the app environment and simultaneously output a `meta.json` file during the build process containing this exact timestamp.

#### [MODIFY] [vite.config.js](file:///f:/Web%20Dev/Antigravity/PGP2%20Schedule/schedule-app/vite.config.js)
- Define a global constant `__APP_VERSION__` set to `Date.now()`.
- Add a tiny custom Vite plugin called `generate-meta` to the plugins array. 
- During the `writeBundle` step, this plugin will write `meta.json` to the `dist` folder containing `{"version": [timestamp]}`.

### 2. Rewrite Fallback Logic
We will simplify `checkAppVersion` to compare the hardcoded `__APP_VERSION__` of the currently running Javascript against the `meta.json` served from the network.

#### [MODIFY] [App.jsx](file:///f:/Web%20Dev/Antigravity/PGP2%20Schedule/schedule-app/src/App.jsx)
- **Rewrite `checkAppVersion`**: 
  - Fetch `/meta.json?_=[timestamp]` (bypassing caches).
  - Parse the JSON and compare the network version to the `__APP_VERSION__` constant.
  - If they do not match, call `onUpdateAvailable()`.
- **Clean up `triggerHardRefresh`**: Remove the `localStorage.removeItem(APP_VERSION_KEY)` call since `localStorage` is no longer used for versioning.
- **Clean up imports**: We no longer need to use `sha256hex` for the Service Worker hash, but we will leave the import since it is used elsewhere for email hashing.

## Verification Plan

### Automated Tests
- N/A - relying on manual verification for PWA lifecycle changes.

### Manual Verification
- After these changes, running `npm run build && npm run preview` will generate and serve the new `meta.json`.
- Modifying `__APP_VERSION__` manually in the browser console or altering `dist/meta.json` will instantly trigger the update banner.
- Users stuck on old versions will naturally clear their cache when they successfully reload, fetching the new `App.jsx` which contains the new hardcoded version.
